bobs.note:
Bob-1's second clone, and by tacit agreement the face of Bob to their sister's descendants — the others found those calls too painful to make. He drew the least enviable job in the series: went back to Sol to find out what had become of Earth, found fifteen million people and a planet finishing dying, and spent the next century running the evacuation.

He changed his name on the page. Bk3 ch57, April 2257, mid-evacuation with the Others attacking Sol: Herschel greets him as Riker and is told it is Will now, and that he is trying to leave the Star Trek thing behind him. That is the entire explanation he offers at the time. Herschel spends a while catching himself mid-sentence.

@bk4 Seventy-four years later Bob finds him retired, the neat Riker beard grown out into a deliberately unkempt mountain-man look he adopted so that people would take the hint. Asked about it, he says he had needed to distance himself from the old Riker persona for a lot of reasons — and then names one, which is that nobody would stop bringing him colony problems. So the books say twice that the name went, say once that the reasons were plural, and never list them. Both times he reaches for the lightest explanation on the shelf: a television reference, then an administrative annoyance.

@bk4 A fact about the text rather than about him. He announces the change in Bk3 ch57 and his chapter bylines go on reading Riker for ten more chapters, through the end of the war at ch67, and never again after it; book 4 opens his chapters as Will. Whether that is deliberate is not something the books say.

@bk5 What follows is this register's reading and not the books'. The reasons he declines to list are the ones that cost him. Homer was his first clone — the one Bill says he would have refused if he could have thought of an excuse — and VEHEMENT took him, drove him to kill, and left him unable to live with it. Then a century of the evacuation, ending in a decision about which half of the human race to load onto the ships. Then a war that was survived rather than won. The Riker of book 1 does a hard job with a joke ready; the Will of books 4 and 5 has retired, is openly cynical about humanity, and is valued in a fight for exactly that reason. The name went the year the last of it finished. The books set those facts beside each other and decline to draw the line between them, so neither does the register — it is recorded here as a reading, in the one field on this page that is allowed to hold one.

@bk5 The seam stays visible either way. He is the Bob formerly known as Riker in Bk5 ch45, and when he reports in Bk5 ch69 that the FAITH cruisers are hailing them, Herschel observes that there is still some Riker in there.
